Windows Registry Editor Version 5.00

; ===================================================================
;  ZenSec context menu installer
; ===================================================================
;  HOW TO USE: right-click this file -> "Install", or run it elevated:
;      reg import install_context_menu.reg
;
;  TWO REAL LIMITATIONS
;  --------------------
;  (a) 'zensec.exe' must be reachable on PATH. The verbs call the bare
;      executable name, so if it is not on PATH Explorer reports
;      "Windows cannot find 'zensec.exe'". Copy it to a PATH directory or
;      edit the two command lines below to use a full path such as
;      "C:\Program Files\ZenSec\zensec.exe".
;  (b) These keys live under HKEY_CLASSES_ROOT\*\shell, which is the
;      merged HKCR view. Editing them affects EVERY user on the machine,
;      and HKCR/HKLM writes need Administrator rights. Import this
;      elevated; to remove the entries, delete the ZenSecEncrypt and
;      ZenSecDecrypt keys.
;
;  WHY NO 'cmd.exe /c' WRAPPER
;  --------------------------
;  The previous version wrapped the call in cmd.exe /c "... \"%1\" ...",
;  which is broken: cmd.exe has no backslash escaping, so the literal
;  \" leaked through as part of the filename and every path with a
;  space failed. The shell passes %1 straight to CreateProcess, so the
;  binary can be invoked directly and the path needs no escaping at all.
;  This is also what makes filenames containing & ( ) % work: they are
;  handed to the process as a single argv entry and never re-parsed by
;  a command interpreter.
;  Trade-off accepted: without cmd.exe there is no 'pause', so the
;  console window closes as soon as ZenSec exits and the user does not
;  see the success/failure output. Double-click the file in an open
;  terminal instead when you need the output.
;
;  NO -keyfile VARIANT: password prompting through the terminal is the
;  intended behaviour here.
;
;  NO -yes FLAG: in an interactive right-click the user should get the
;  "File already exists. Overwrite? [y/N]" prompt.
;
;  Escaping rule for .reg string values: only the double quote needs to
;  be written as \". Backslashes in paths are literal, so write C:\dir
;  and not C:\\dir.
; ===================================================================

[HKEY_CLASSES_ROOT\*\shell\ZenSecEncrypt]
@="Encrypt with ZenSec"
"Icon"="zensec.exe"

[HKEY_CLASSES_ROOT\*\shell\ZenSecEncrypt\command]
@="\"zensec.exe\" -encrypt -file \"%1\""

[HKEY_CLASSES_ROOT\*\shell\ZenSecDecrypt]
@="Decrypt with ZenSec"
"Icon"="zensec.exe"

[HKEY_CLASSES_ROOT\*\shell\ZenSecDecrypt\command]
@="\"zensec.exe\" -decrypt -file \"%1\""